    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2035886069850239134",
      "url": "https://x.com/FatherChrisVor1/status/2035886069850239134",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/FatherChrisVor1/status/2035886069850239134",
      "text": "Right now, much of the conversation in the United States about the war with Iran is focused on rising gas and diesel prices, and what it’s going to cost us at the store. And yes, that matters.\n\nBut look beyond us.\n\nCountries like the Philippines, which import the vast majority of their oil, much of it tied to the Strait of Hormuz, are being hit far harder. Fuel prices have surged to historic levels. Inflation is rising. The currency is weakening. Transport systems are under strain, and governments are being forced into emergency measures, even reducing work schedules to conserve energy.\n\nAnd it’s not just the Philippines.\n\nAcross parts of Asia and Africa, nations that are not involved in this conflict are facing real disruption. This isn’t just about higher prices. It’s about whether transportation holds, whether food supply chains stay intact, whether daily life continues without breakdown.\n\nWe talk about inconvenience. Others are dealing with instability.\n\nWar does not stay where it starts.",
